INT. FARRYNER'S BAKEHOUSE, PUDDING LANE - NIGHT

September, 1666. A low, smoke-blackened bakehouse. Ovens glow. Flour dust hangs in the air like fog. Barrels, kneading troughs, a ladder to a loft.

THOMAS FARRYNER (40s, baker, forearms scarred by decades of oven doors) rakes embers with an iron peel. A sleepy APPRENTICE named EDMUND BRANDON (14, flour in his hair) kneads a lump of dough on the trough.

Thomas straightens, wipes his brow, and looks at the oven mouth. A stray ember jumps out onto the floor. He stamps it. Another leaps. He stamps again, then peels the oven door shut with his foot while Edmund turns, rubs his eyes, reaches for a rag, and misses the barrel behind him.

THOMAS
Mind the faggots, boy.

EDMUND
Yes, Master.

THOMAS
Stacked too near.

EDMUND
They're always there.

THOMAS
They're always there because I've never lit it this hot.

Edmund looks at the oven. At his master. At the bundle of dry kindling heaped against the wall.

EDMUND
Master, it's very hot.

THOMAS
It's the dry summer.

EDMUND
The thatch is hot too. I can feel it from here.

Thomas looks up. A thread of smoke is drifting out of a gap in the rafters. He climbs the ladder two rungs at a time. Edmund grabs a bucket, dips it in the trough, and splashes the wall. It hisses.

THOMAS
(from the loft)
Water! More!

EDMUND
There isn't more, Master. The trough is dough.

THOMAS
Then bring the ale!

Edmund drags a barrel, tips it, lunges for the loft with the first pitcher full. Thomas stamps at something in the straw. The barrel rolls across the floor. Edmund slips on it, lands on his back, the pitcher still raised, not a drop spilled.

THOMAS (CONT'D)
Boy!

EDMUND
Here, Master!

Thomas descends, lifts the boy by the collar, sets him on his feet, snatches the pitcher, and throws it up through the hatch. A hiss. Orange light flares at the edge of the loft. Both stare.

THOMAS
Out.

EDMUND
The bread!

THOMAS
Out, Edmund!

He shoves the boy toward the door and follows, and as they spill onto the street a bell begins to ring somewhere down the lane, one bell, then two.
